window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2017-12-3", "source": "2017年12月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay commenting on the saying \"Help others, and you will be helped when you are in need.\" You can cite examples to illustrate your views. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "There is profound wisdom in the saying that if we help others, we will be helped when we are in need. Far\nfrom being empty idealism, this remark captures a genuine law of human relationships: kindness given tends to\nreturn to its giver. In my view, the saying holds true, and it is worth taking to heart.\nThe reason lies in the reciprocal nature of society. When we offer help to those around us, we build trust and\ngoodwill; these bonds, in turn, become the support we can rely on when trouble strikes. A student who\npatiently explains a difficult problem to a classmate, for example, will often find that same classmate ready to\nlend a hand before the next exam. Because no one can succeed entirely alone, the willingness to give first is\nwhat secures help later.\nTherefore, we should treat helping others not as a loss but as a wise investment. If each of us reaches out today,\na warmer and more supportive world awaits us all tomorrow."}, "translation": {"prompt": "洞庭湖位于湖南省东北部，面积很大，但湖水很浅。洞庭湖是长江的蓄洪池，湖的大校很大程度上取决于季节变化，湖北和湖南两省因其与湖的相对位置而得名，湖北意为\"湖的北边\"，而湖南则为\"湖的南边\"。洞庭湖作为龙舟赛的发源地，在中国文化中享有盛名。据说龙舟赛始于洞庭湖东岸。为的是搜寻楚国爱国诗人屈原的遗体。龙舟赛与洞庭湖及周边的美景，每年都吸引着成千上万来自全国和世界各地的游客。", "sample": "Dongting Lake lies in the northeast of Hunan Province, with a large area but shallow water. It serves as a flood basin of the Yangtze River, and its size largely depends on seasonal changes. The provinces of Hubei and Hunan are named after their positions relative to the lake, with Hubei meaning \"north of the lake\" and Hunan meaning \"south of the lake\". As the birthplace of dragon boat racing, Dongting Lake enjoys a great reputation in Chinese culture. It is said that dragon boat racing originated on the eastern shore of Dongting Lake to search for the body of Qu Yuan, a patriotic poet of the State of Chu. The dragon boat race, together with the beautiful scenery of Dongting Lake and its surroundings, attracts thousands of tourists from all over the country and the world every year."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "Many European countries have been making the shift to electric vehicles and Germany has just stated that they plan to ban the sale of vehicles using gasoline and diesel as fuel by 2030. The country is also planning to reduce its carbon footprint by 80-95% by 2050,  __26__  a shift to green energy in the country. Effectively, the ban will include the registration of new cars in the country as they will not allow any gasoline  __27__  vehicle to be registered after 2030.\nPart of the reason this ban is being discussed and  __28__  is because energy officials see that they will not reach their emissions goals by 2050 if they do not  __29__  a large portion of vehicle emissions. The country is still  __30__  that it will meet its emissions goals, like reducing emissions by 40% by 2020, but the  __31__  of electric cars in the country has not occurred as fast as expected.\nOther efforts to increase the use of electric vehicles include plans to build over 1 million hybrid and electric car battery changing stations across the country. By 2030, Germany plans on having over 6 million charging stations  __32__  . According to the International Business Times, electric car sales are expected to increase as Volkswagen is still recovering from its emissions scandal.\nThere are  __33__  around 155,000 registered hybrid and electric vehicles on German roads, dwarfed by the 45 million gasoline and diesel cars driving there now. As countries continue setting goals of reducing emissions, greater steps need to be taken to have a  __34__  effect on the surrounding environment. While the efforts are certainly not  __35__  , the results of such bans will likely only start to be seen by generations down the line, bettering the world for the future.", "matchIntro": "Apple's Stance Highlights a More Confrontational Tech Industry", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "The battle between Apple and law enforcement officials over unlocking a terrorist's smartphone is the culmination of a slow turning of the tables between the technology industry and the United States government."}, {"label": "B", "text": "After revelations by the former National Security Agency contractor Edward J. Snowden in 2013 that the government both cozied up to(讨好) certain tech companies and hacked into others to gain access to private data on an enormous scale, tech giants began to recognize the United States government as a hostile actor. But if the confrontation has crystallized in this latest battle, it may already be heading toward a predictable conclusion: In the long run, the tech companies are destined to emerge victorious."}, {"label": "C", "text": "It may not seem that way at the moment. On the one side, you have the United States government's mighty legal and security apparatus fighting for data of the most sympathetic sort: the secrets buried in a dead mass murderer's phone. The action stems from a federal court order issued on Tuesday requiring Apple to help the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) to unlock an iPhone used by one of the two attackers who killed 14 people in San Bernardino, California, in December."}, {"label": "D", "text": "In the other corner is the world's most valuable company, whose chief executive, Timothy Cook, has said he will appeal the court's order. Apple argues that it is fighting to preserve a principle that most of us who are addicted to our smartphones can defend: Weaken a single iPhone so that its contents can be viewed by the American government and you risk weakening all iPhones for any government intruder, anywhere."}, {"label": "E", "text": "There will probably be months of legal confrontation, and it is not at all clear which side will prevail in court, nor in the battle for public opinion and legislative favor. Yet underlying all of this is a simple dynamic: Apple, Google, Facebook and other companies hold most of the cards in this confrontation. They have our data, and their businesses depend on the global public's collective belief that they will do everything they can to protect that data."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Any crack in that front could be fatal for tech companies that must operate worldwide. If Apple is forced to open up an iPhone for an American law enforcement investigation, what is to prevent it from doing so for a request from the Russians or the Iranians? If Apple is forced to write code that lets the FBI get into the iPhone 5c used by Syed Rizwan Farook, the male attacker in the San Bernardino attack, who would be responsible if some hacker got hold of that code and broke into its other devices?"}, {"label": "G", "text": "Apple's stance on these issues emerged post-Snowden, when the company started putting in place a series of technologies that, by default, make use of encryption(加密) to limit access to people's data. More than that, Apple—and, in different ways, other tech companies, including Google, Facebook, Twitter and Microsoft—have made their opposition to the government's claims a point of corporate pride."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Apple's emerging global brand is privacy; it has staked its corporate reputation, not to mention the investment of considerable technical and financial resources, on limiting the sort of mass surveillance that was uncovered by Mr. Snowden. So now, for many cases involving governmental intrusions into data, once-lonely privacy advocates find themselves fighting alongside the most powerful company in the world."}, {"label": "I", "text": "\"A comparison point is in the 1990s battles over encryption,\" said Kurt Opsahl, general counsel of the Electronic Frontier Foundation, a privacy watchdog group. \"Then you had a few companies involved, but not one of the largest companies in the world coming out with a lengthy and impassioned post, like we saw yesterday from Timothy Cook. Its profile has really been raised.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "Apple and other tech companies hold another ace: the technical means to keep making their devices more and more inaccessible. Note that Apple's public opposition to the government's request is itself a hindrance to mass government intrusion. And to get at the contents of a single iPhone, the government says it needs a court order and Apple's help to write new code; in earlier versions of the iPhone, ones that were created before Apple found religion on(热衷于) privacy, the FBI might have been able to break into the device by itself."}, {"label": "K", "text": "You can expect that noose(束缚) to continue to tighten. Experts said that whether or not Apple loses this specific case, measures that it could put into place in the future will almost certainly be able to further limit the government's reach."}, {"label": "L", "text": "That is not to say that the outcome of the San Bernardino case is insignificant. As Apple and several security experts have argued, an order compelling Apple to write software that gives the FBI access to the iPhone in question would establish an unsettling precedent. The order essentially asks Apple to hack its own devices, and once it is in place, the precedent could be used to justify law enforcement efforts to get around encryption technologies in other investigations far removed from national security threats."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Once armed with a method for gaining access to iPhones, the government could ask to use it proactively(先发制人地), before a suspected terrorist attack—leaving Apple in a bind as to whether to comply or risk an attack and suffer a public-relations nightmare. \"This is a brand new move in the war against encryption,\" Mr. Opsahl said. \"We have had plenty of debates in Congress and the media over whether the government should have a backdoor, and this is an end run(迂回战术) around that—here they come with an order to create that backdoor.\""}, {"label": "N", "text": "Yet it is worth noting that even if Apple ultimately loses this case, it has plenty of technical means to close a backdoor over time. \"If they are anywhere near worth their salt as engineers, I bet they are rethinking their threat model as we speak,\" said Jonathan Zdziarski, a digital expert who studies the iPhone and its vulnerabilities."}, {"label": "O", "text": "One relatively simple fix, Mr. Zdziarski said, would be for Apple to modify future versions of the iPhone to require a user to enter a passcode before the phone will accept the sort of modified operating system that the FBI wants Apple to create. That way, Apple could not unilaterally introduce a code that weakens the iPhone—a user would have to consent to it."}, {"label": "P", "text": "\"Nothing is 100 percent hacker-proof,\" Mr. Zdziarski said, but he pointed out that the judge's order in this case required Apple to provide \"reasonable security assistance\" to unlock Mr. Farook's phone. If Apple alters the security model of future iPhones so that even its own engineers' \"reasonable assistance\" will not be able to crack a given device when compelled by the government, a precedent set in this case might lose its lasting force. In other words, even if the FBI wins this case, in the long run, it loses."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "At the base of a mountain in Tanzania's Gregory Rift, Lake Natron burns bright red, surrounded by the remains of animals that were unfortunate enough to fall into the salty water. Bats, swallows and more are chemically preserved in the pose in which they perished, sealed in the deposits of sodium carbonate in the water. The lake's landscape is bizarre and deadly—and made even more so by the fact that it's the place where nearly 75 percent of the world's flamingos(火烈鸟) are born.\nThe water is so corrosive that it can burn the skin and eyes of unadapted animals. Flamingos, however, are the only species that actually makes life in the midst of all that death. Once every three or four years, when conditions are right, the lake is covered with the pink birds as they stop flight to breed. Three-quarters of the world's flamingos fly over from other salt lakes in the Rift Valley and nest on salt-crystal islands that appear when the water is at a specific level—too high and the birds can't build their nests, too low and predators can move briskly across the lake bed and attack. When the water hits the right level, the baby birds are kept safe from predators by a corrosive ditch.\n\"Flamingos have evolved very leathery skin on their legs so they can tolerate the salt water,\" says David Harper, a professor at the University of Leicester. \"Humans cannot, and would die if their legs were exposed for any length of time.\" So far this year, water levels have been too high for the flamingos to nest.\nSome fish, too, have had limited success vacationing at the lake as less salty lagoons(泻湖) form on the outer edges from hot springs flowing into Lake Natron. Three species of tilapia(罗非鱼) thrive there part-time. \"Fish have a refuge in the streams and can expand into the lagoons when the lake is low and the lagoons are separate,\" Harper said. \"All the lagoons join when the lake is high and fish must retreat to their stream refuges or die.\" Otherwise, no fish are able to survive in the naturally toxic lake.\nThis unique ecosystem may soon be under pressure. The Tanzanian government has once again started mining the lake for soda ash, used for making chemicals, glass and detergents. Although the planned operation will be located more than 40 miles away, drawing the soda ash in through pipelines, conservationists worry it could still upset the natural water cycle and breeding grounds. For now, though, life prevails—even in a lake that kills almost everything it touches.", "47": "At the base of a mountain in Tanzania's Gregory Rift, Lake Natron burns bright red, surrounded by the remains of animals that were unfortunate enough to fall into the salty water. Bats, swallows and more are chemically preserved in the pose in which they perished, sealed in the deposits of sodium carbonate in the water. The lake's landscape is bizarre and deadly—and made even more so by the fact that it's the place where nearly 75 percent of the world's flamingos(火烈鸟) are born.\nThe water is so corrosive that it can burn the skin and eyes of unadapted animals. Flamingos, however, are the only species that actually makes life in the midst of all that death. Once every three or four years, when conditions are right, the lake is covered with the pink birds as they stop flight to breed. Three-quarters of the world's flamingos fly over from other salt lakes in the Rift Valley and nest on salt-crystal islands that appear when the water is at a specific level—too high and the birds can't build their nests, too low and predators can move briskly across the lake bed and attack. When the water hits the right level, the baby birds are kept safe from predators by a corrosive ditch.\n\"Flamingos have evolved very leathery skin on their legs so they can tolerate the salt water,\" says David Harper, a professor at the University of Leicester. \"Humans cannot, and would die if their legs were exposed for any length of time.\" So far this year, water levels have been too high for the flamingos to nest.\nSome fish, too, have had limited success vacationing at the lake as less salty lagoons(泻湖) form on the outer edges from hot springs flowing into Lake Natron. Three species of tilapia(罗非鱼) thrive there part-time. \"Fish have a refuge in the streams and can expand into the lagoons when the lake is low and the lagoons are separate,\" Harper said. \"All the lagoons join when the lake is high and fish must retreat to their stream refuges or die.\" Otherwise, no fish are able to survive in the naturally toxic lake.\nThis unique ecosystem may soon be under pressure. The Tanzanian government has once again started mining the lake for soda ash, used for making chemicals, glass and detergents. Although the planned operation will be located more than 40 miles away, drawing the soda ash in through pipelines, conservationists worry it could still upset the natural water cycle and breeding grounds. For now, though, life prevails—even in a lake that kills almost everything it touches.", "48": "At the base of a mountain in Tanzania's Gregory Rift, Lake Natron burns bright red, surrounded by the remains of animals that were unfortunate enough to fall into the salty water. Bats, swallows and more are chemically preserved in the pose in which they perished, sealed in the deposits of sodium carbonate in the water. The lake's landscape is bizarre and deadly—and made even more so by the fact that it's the place where nearly 75 percent of the world's flamingos(火烈鸟) are born.\nThe water is so corrosive that it can burn the skin and eyes of unadapted animals. Flamingos, however, are the only species that actually makes life in the midst of all that death. Once every three or four years, when conditions are right, the lake is covered with the pink birds as they stop flight to breed. Three-quarters of the world's flamingos fly over from other salt lakes in the Rift Valley and nest on salt-crystal islands that appear when the water is at a specific level—too high and the birds can't build their nests, too low and predators can move briskly across the lake bed and attack. When the water hits the right level, the baby birds are kept safe from predators by a corrosive ditch.\n\"Flamingos have evolved very leathery skin on their legs so they can tolerate the salt water,\" says David Harper, a professor at the University of Leicester. \"Humans cannot, and would die if their legs were exposed for any length of time.\" So far this year, water levels have been too high for the flamingos to nest.\nSome fish, too, have had limited success vacationing at the lake as less salty lagoons(泻湖) form on the outer edges from hot springs flowing into Lake Natron. Three species of tilapia(罗非鱼) thrive there part-time. \"Fish have a refuge in the streams and can expand into the lagoons when the lake is low and the lagoons are separate,\" Harper said. \"All the lagoons join when the lake is high and fish must retreat to their stream refuges or die.\" Otherwise, no fish are able to survive in the naturally toxic lake.\nThis unique ecosystem may soon be under pressure. The Tanzanian government has once again started mining the lake for soda ash, used for making chemicals, glass and detergents. Although the planned operation will be located more than 40 miles away, drawing the soda ash in through pipelines, conservationists worry it could still upset the natural water cycle and breeding grounds. For now, though, life prevails—even in a lake that kills almost everything it touches.", "49": "At the base of a mountain in Tanzania's Gregory Rift, Lake Natron burns bright red, surrounded by the remains of animals that were unfortunate enough to fall into the salty water. Bats, swallows and more are chemically preserved in the pose in which they perished, sealed in the deposits of sodium carbonate in the water. The lake's landscape is bizarre and deadly—and made even more so by the fact that it's the place where nearly 75 percent of the world's flamingos(火烈鸟) are born.\nThe water is so corrosive that it can burn the skin and eyes of unadapted animals. Flamingos, however, are the only species that actually makes life in the midst of all that death. Once every three or four years, when conditions are right, the lake is covered with the pink birds as they stop flight to breed. Three-quarters of the world's flamingos fly over from other salt lakes in the Rift Valley and nest on salt-crystal islands that appear when the water is at a specific level—too high and the birds can't build their nests, too low and predators can move briskly across the lake bed and attack. When the water hits the right level, the baby birds are kept safe from predators by a corrosive ditch.\n\"Flamingos have evolved very leathery skin on their legs so they can tolerate the salt water,\" says David Harper, a professor at the University of Leicester. \"Humans cannot, and would die if their legs were exposed for any length of time.\" So far this year, water levels have been too high for the flamingos to nest.\nSome fish, too, have had limited success vacationing at the lake as less salty lagoons(泻湖) form on the outer edges from hot springs flowing into Lake Natron. Three species of tilapia(罗非鱼) thrive there part-time. \"Fish have a refuge in the streams and can expand into the lagoons when the lake is low and the lagoons are separate,\" Harper said. \"All the lagoons join when the lake is high and fish must retreat to their stream refuges or die.\" Otherwise, no fish are able to survive in the naturally toxic lake.\nThis unique ecosystem may soon be under pressure. The Tanzanian government has once again started mining the lake for soda ash, used for making chemicals, glass and detergents. Although the planned operation will be located more than 40 miles away, drawing the soda ash in through pipelines, conservationists worry it could still upset the natural water cycle and breeding grounds. For now, though, life prevails—even in a lake that kills almost everything it touches.", "50": "At the base of a mountain in Tanzania's Gregory Rift, Lake Natron burns bright red, surrounded by the remains of animals that were unfortunate enough to fall into the salty water. Bats, swallows and more are chemically preserved in the pose in which they perished, sealed in the deposits of sodium carbonate in the water. The lake's landscape is bizarre and deadly—and made even more so by the fact that it's the place where nearly 75 percent of the world's flamingos(火烈鸟) are born.\nThe water is so corrosive that it can burn the skin and eyes of unadapted animals. Flamingos, however, are the only species that actually makes life in the midst of all that death. Once every three or four years, when conditions are right, the lake is covered with the pink birds as they stop flight to breed. Three-quarters of the world's flamingos fly over from other salt lakes in the Rift Valley and nest on salt-crystal islands that appear when the water is at a specific level—too high and the birds can't build their nests, too low and predators can move briskly across the lake bed and attack. When the water hits the right level, the baby birds are kept safe from predators by a corrosive ditch.\n\"Flamingos have evolved very leathery skin on their legs so they can tolerate the salt water,\" says David Harper, a professor at the University of Leicester. \"Humans cannot, and would die if their legs were exposed for any length of time.\" So far this year, water levels have been too high for the flamingos to nest.\nSome fish, too, have had limited success vacationing at the lake as less salty lagoons(泻湖) form on the outer edges from hot springs flowing into Lake Natron. Three species of tilapia(罗非鱼) thrive there part-time. \"Fish have a refuge in the streams and can expand into the lagoons when the lake is low and the lagoons are separate,\" Harper said. \"All the lagoons join when the lake is high and fish must retreat to their stream refuges or die.\" Otherwise, no fish are able to survive in the naturally toxic lake.\nThis unique ecosystem may soon be under pressure. The Tanzanian government has once again started mining the lake for soda ash, used for making chemicals, glass and detergents. Although the planned operation will be located more than 40 miles away, drawing the soda ash in through pipelines, conservationists worry it could still upset the natural water cycle and breeding grounds. For now, though, life prevails—even in a lake that kills almost everything it touches.", "51": "It is the season for some frantic last-minute math—across the country, employees of all stripes are counting backward in an attempt to figure out just how much paid time-off they have left in their reserves. More of them, though, will skip those calculations altogether and just power through the holidays into 2017: More than half of American workers don't use up all of their allotted vacation days each year.\nNot so long ago, people would have turned up their noses at that kind of dedication to the job. As marketing professors Silvia Bellezza, Neeru Paharia, and Anat Keinan recently explained in Harvard Business Review (HBR), leisure time was once seen as an indicator of high social status, something attainable only for those at the top. Since the middle of the 20th century, though, things have turned the opposite way—these days, punishing hours at your desk, rather than days off, are seen as the mark of someone important.\nIn a series of several experiments, the researchers illustrated just how much we've come to admire busyness, or at least the appearance of it. Volunteers read two passages, one about a man who led a life of leisure and another about a man who was over-worked and over-scheduled; when asked to determine which of the two had a higher social status, the majority of the participants said the latter. The same held true for people who used products that implied they were short on time: In one experiment, for example, customers of the grocery-delivery service Peapod were seen as of higher status than people who shopped at grocery stores that were equally expensive; in another, people wearing wireless headphones were considered further up on the social ladder than those wearing regular headphones, even when both were just used to listen to music.\nIn part, the authors wrote in HBR, this pattern may have to do with the way work itself has changed over the past several decades.\nWe think that the shift from leisure-as-status to busyness-as-status may be linked to the development of knowledge-intensive economics. In such economies, individuals who possess the human capital characteristics that employers or clients value (e.g., competence and ambition) are expected to be in high demand and short supply on the job market. Thus, by telling others that we are busy and working all the time, we are implicitly suggesting that we are sought after, which enhances our perceived status.\nEven if you feel tempted to sacrifice your own vacation days for fake busyness, though, at least consider leaving your weekends unscheduled. It's for your own good.", "52": "It is the season for some frantic last-minute math—across the country, employees of all stripes are counting backward in an attempt to figure out just how much paid time-off they have left in their reserves. More of them, though, will skip those calculations altogether and just power through the holidays into 2017: More than half of American workers don't use up all of their allotted vacation days each year.\nNot so long ago, people would have turned up their noses at that kind of dedication to the job. As marketing professors Silvia Bellezza, Neeru Paharia, and Anat Keinan recently explained in Harvard Business Review (HBR), leisure time was once seen as an indicator of high social status, something attainable only for those at the top. Since the middle of the 20th century, though, things have turned the opposite way—these days, punishing hours at your desk, rather than days off, are seen as the mark of someone important.\nIn a series of several experiments, the researchers illustrated just how much we've come to admire busyness, or at least the appearance of it. Volunteers read two passages, one about a man who led a life of leisure and another about a man who was over-worked and over-scheduled; when asked to determine which of the two had a higher social status, the majority of the participants said the latter. The same held true for people who used products that implied they were short on time: In one experiment, for example, customers of the grocery-delivery service Peapod were seen as of higher status than people who shopped at grocery stores that were equally expensive; in another, people wearing wireless headphones were considered further up on the social ladder than those wearing regular headphones, even when both were just used to listen to music.\nIn part, the authors wrote in HBR, this pattern may have to do with the way work itself has changed over the past several decades.\nWe think that the shift from leisure-as-status to busyness-as-status may be linked to the development of knowledge-intensive economics. In such economies, individuals who possess the human capital characteristics that employers or clients value (e.g., competence and ambition) are expected to be in high demand and short supply on the job market. Thus, by telling others that we are busy and working all the time, we are implicitly suggesting that we are sought after, which enhances our perceived status.\nEven if you feel tempted to sacrifice your own vacation days for fake busyness, though, at least consider leaving your weekends unscheduled. It's for your own good.", "53": "It is the season for some frantic last-minute math—across the country, employees of all stripes are counting backward in an attempt to figure out just how much paid time-off they have left in their reserves. More of them, though, will skip those calculations altogether and just power through the holidays into 2017: More than half of American workers don't use up all of their allotted vacation days each year.\nNot so long ago, people would have turned up their noses at that kind of dedication to the job. As marketing professors Silvia Bellezza, Neeru Paharia, and Anat Keinan recently explained in Harvard Business Review (HBR), leisure time was once seen as an indicator of high social status, something attainable only for those at the top. Since the middle of the 20th century, though, things have turned the opposite way—these days, punishing hours at your desk, rather than days off, are seen as the mark of someone important.\nIn a series of several experiments, the researchers illustrated just how much we've come to admire busyness, or at least the appearance of it. Volunteers read two passages, one about a man who led a life of leisure and another about a man who was over-worked and over-scheduled; when asked to determine which of the two had a higher social status, the majority of the participants said the latter. The same held true for people who used products that implied they were short on time: In one experiment, for example, customers of the grocery-delivery service Peapod were seen as of higher status than people who shopped at grocery stores that were equally expensive; in another, people wearing wireless headphones were considered further up on the social ladder than those wearing regular headphones, even when both were just used to listen to music.\nIn part, the authors wrote in HBR, this pattern may have to do with the way work itself has changed over the past several decades.\nWe think that the shift from leisure-as-status to busyness-as-status may be linked to the development of knowledge-intensive economics. In such economies, individuals who possess the human capital characteristics that employers or clients value (e.g., competence and ambition) are expected to be in high demand and short supply on the job market. Thus, by telling others that we are busy and working all the time, we are implicitly suggesting that we are sought after, which enhances our perceived status.\nEven if you feel tempted to sacrifice your own vacation days for fake busyness, though, at least consider leaving your weekends unscheduled. It's for your own good.", "54": "It is the season for some frantic last-minute math—across the country, employees of all stripes are counting backward in an attempt to figure out just how much paid time-off they have left in their reserves. More of them, though, will skip those calculations altogether and just power through the holidays into 2017: More than half of American workers don't use up all of their allotted vacation days each year.\nNot so long ago, people would have turned up their noses at that kind of dedication to the job. As marketing professors Silvia Bellezza, Neeru Paharia, and Anat Keinan recently explained in Harvard Business Review (HBR), leisure time was once seen as an indicator of high social status, something attainable only for those at the top. Since the middle of the 20th century, though, things have turned the opposite way—these days, punishing hours at your desk, rather than days off, are seen as the mark of someone important.\nIn a series of several experiments, the researchers illustrated just how much we've come to admire busyness, or at least the appearance of it. Volunteers read two passages, one about a man who led a life of leisure and another about a man who was over-worked and over-scheduled; when asked to determine which of the two had a higher social status, the majority of the participants said the latter. The same held true for people who used products that implied they were short on time: In one experiment, for example, customers of the grocery-delivery service Peapod were seen as of higher status than people who shopped at grocery stores that were equally expensive; in another, people wearing wireless headphones were considered further up on the social ladder than those wearing regular headphones, even when both were just used to listen to music.\nIn part, the authors wrote in HBR, this pattern may have to do with the way work itself has changed over the past several decades.\nWe think that the shift from leisure-as-status to busyness-as-status may be linked to the development of knowledge-intensive economics. In such economies, individuals who possess the human capital characteristics that employers or clients value (e.g., competence and ambition) are expected to be in high demand and short supply on the job market. Thus, by telling others that we are busy and working all the time, we are implicitly suggesting that we are sought after, which enhances our perceived status.\nEven if you feel tempted to sacrifice your own vacation days for fake busyness, though, at least consider leaving your weekends unscheduled. It's for your own good.", "55": "It is the season for some frantic last-minute math—across the country, employees of all stripes are counting backward in an attempt to figure out just how much paid time-off they have left in their reserves. More of them, though, will skip those calculations altogether and just power through the holidays into 2017: More than half of American workers don't use up all of their allotted vacation days each year.\nNot so long ago, people would have turned up their noses at that kind of dedication to the job. As marketing professors Silvia Bellezza, Neeru Paharia, and Anat Keinan recently explained in Harvard Business Review (HBR), leisure time was once seen as an indicator of high social status, something attainable only for those at the top. Since the middle of the 20th century, though, things have turned the opposite way—these days, punishing hours at your desk, rather than days off, are seen as the mark of someone important.\nIn a series of several experiments, the researchers illustrated just how much we've come to admire busyness, or at least the appearance of it. Volunteers read two passages, one about a man who led a life of leisure and another about a man who was over-worked and over-scheduled; when asked to determine which of the two had a higher social status, the majority of the participants said the latter. The same held true for people who used products that implied they were short on time: In one experiment, for example, customers of the grocery-delivery service Peapod were seen as of higher status than people who shopped at grocery stores that were equally expensive; in another, people wearing wireless headphones were considered further up on the social ladder than those wearing regular headphones, even when both were just used to listen to music.\nIn part, the authors wrote in HBR, this pattern may have to do with the way work itself has changed over the past several decades.\nWe think that the shift from leisure-as-status to busyness-as-status may be linked to the development of knowledge-intensive economics. In such economies, individuals who possess the human capital characteristics that employers or clients value (e.g., competence and ambition) are expected to be in high demand and short supply on the job market. Thus, by telling others that we are busy and working all the time, we are implicitly suggesting that we are sought after, which enhances our perceived status.\nEven if you feel tempted to sacrifice your own vacation days for fake busyness, though, at least consider leaving your weekends unscheduled. It's for your own good."}, "questions": [{"id": "2017-12-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acceptance", "B) currently", "C) disrupting", "D) eliminate", "E) exhaust", "F) futile", "G) hopeful", "H) implemented", "I) incidentally", "J) installed", "K) noticeable", "L) powered", "M) restoration", "N) skeptical", "O) sparking"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) sparking\n动词\n【词性槽】逗号后悬垂分词作伴随状语，需 -ing 形式动词\n【依据】spark a shift = 引发一次转向，固定搭配\nspark + shift/change/debate/interest 是英语高频搭配，意为「引发、催生」；对应 The\ncountry is also planning to reduce its carbon footprint by 80-95% by 2050, __(26)__\na shift to green energy in the country. 表「减排目标催生绿色能源转向」，语义因果\n清晰。\n【竞争词】C) disrupting 义反 + 搭配错位\ndisrupt a shift 是「打断一次转向」，与「减排带动绿色转型」的正向因果相反，且与下文「政\n府推动」的积极语气不一致。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·逗号后悬垂分词作伴随状语，需 -ing"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acceptance", "B) currently", "C) disrupting", "D) eliminate", "E) exhaust", "F) futile", "G) hopeful", "H) implemented", "I) incidentally", "J) installed", "K) noticeable", "L) powered", "M) restoration", "N) skeptical", "O) sparking"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) powered\n形容词\n【词性槽】gasoline ___ vehicle 中作复合形容词修饰名词\n【依据】gasoline-powered = 以汽油为动力的，固定搭配\n对应 they will not allow any gasoline __(27)__ vehicle to be registered after 2030：与\n全文反复出现的 vehicles using gasoline and diesel as fuel 意思完全一致，powered 是\n「由…驱动」的过去分词作形容词，与 gasoline 复合自然贴合「禁售燃油车」语境。\n【竞争词】C) disrupting 义反，与禁售燃油车背景不符\ndisrupting 表示「扰乱、颠覆」，gasoline-disrupting vehicle 既无此搭配也与文意（汽油车\n被淘汰）相反。\nF) futile 义不通\nfutile 意为「徒劳的」，gasoline-futile vehicle 不成立。\nG) hopeful 搭配与情感色彩均不合\nhopeful 修饰人或前景，不能修饰汽车的动力来源。\nH) implemented 对象不对\nimplemented 修饰的是政策/计划这类抽象事物，不修饰 vehicle。\nJ) installed 搭配错位\ninstalled 通常说「安装的设备」，gasoline-installed vehicle 不是英文表达。\nK) noticeable 义不切题\nnoticeable 是「明显的」，与「动力来源」无关。\nN) skeptical 对象不对\nskeptical 用于「人」对某事持怀疑态度，不能修饰 vehicle。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·gasoline ___ vehicle"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acceptance", "B) currently", "C) disrupting", "D) eliminate", "E) exhaust", "F) futile", "G) hopeful", "H) implemented", "I) incidentally", "J) installed", "K) noticeable", "L) powered", "M) restoration", "N) skeptical", "O) sparking"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) implemented\n动词\n【词性槽】being discussed and ___ 并列过去分词\n【依据】implement a ban = 实施禁令，常见搭配\nPart of the reason this ban is being discussed and __(28)__ is because energy officials\nsee that they will not reach their emissions goals by 2050 if they do not __(29)__ a\nlarge portion of vehicle emissions.：禁令被「讨论和实施」，implement a ban/policy\n是固定搭配，递进自然。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·being discussed and"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acceptance", "B) currently", "C) disrupting", "D) eliminate", "E) exhaust", "F) futile", "G) hopeful", "H) implemented", "I) incidentally", "J) installed", "K) noticeable", "L) powered", "M) restoration", "N) skeptical", "O) sparking"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) eliminate\n动词\n【词性槽】情态/助动词 do not + 动词原形\n【依据】eliminate emissions = 消除排放，正合减排目标\n对应 they will not reach their emissions goals by 2050 if they do not __(29)__ a large\nportion of vehicle emissions：句意为「若不消除大部分车辆尾气排放，就达不到目\n标」，eliminate 与 emissions goals 语义闭环。\n【竞争词】E) exhaust 义反 + 形近陷阱\nexhaust 作动词是「耗尽」（耗尽某资源），名词义「废气」正好关联本文话题，是命题人放出\n的形近/题域诱饵；但放在 do not exhaust a large portion of vehicle emissions「不耗尽大部\n分车辆排放」于逻辑上荒谬——你不会「用尽」排放。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— exhaust 与 emissions 同属「尾气」话题的形 + 域双重迷\n惑。they will not reach their emissions goals by 2050 if they do not __(29)__ a large\nportion of vehicle emissions 里看到 vehicle emissions，许多考生直接联想到 exhaust\n（名词义「废气」）就填上；但本空要的是动词原形，exhaust 作动词是「耗尽」，与减排\n逻辑完全相反。防坑：填动词时先盯死动词义而非词面话题。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态/助动词 do not + 动词原形"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acceptance", "B) currently", "C) disrupting", "D) eliminate", "E) exhaust", "F) futile", "G) hopeful", "H) implemented", "I) incidentally", "J) installed", "K) noticeable", "L) powered", "M) restoration", "N) skeptical", "O) sparking"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) hopeful\n形容词\n【词性槽】is still ___ that 从句，表语形容词带 that 同位语从句\n【依据】下文转折 but，主句须正面 → hopeful\nThe country is still __(30)__ that it will meet its emissions goals, like reducing\nemissions by 40% by 2020, but the __(31)__ of electric cars in the country has not\noccurred as fast as expected.：but 之后是负面消息，按「正-但-负」逻辑，but 之前必\n正面，故选 hopeful「乐观相信能达到目标」。\n【竞争词】N) skeptical 极性反\n若填 skeptical「怀疑」，再用 but 转到「电动车普及没那么快」则成「负-但-负」，逻辑断\n裂；且下一段还在罗列政府的积极举措，与「政府自己怀疑能否达标」矛盾。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— be + 形容词 + that 结构里 skeptical 与 hopeful 都合语\n法。考生若只看 The country is still __(30)__ that it will meet its emissions goals, like\nreducing emissions by 40% by 2020, but the __(31)__ of electric cars in the country\nhas not occurred as fast as expected. 半句，hopeful 与 skeptical 都讲得通；必须读到\nbut「电动车普及没那么快」才能用极性反推选 hopeful。防坑：见 but 就要回看前句、对\n齐极性。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·is still ___ that 从句"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acceptance", "B) currently", "C) disrupting", "D) eliminate", "E) exhaust", "F) futile", "G) hopeful", "H) implemented", "I) incidentally", "J) installed", "K) noticeable", "L) powered", "M) restoration", "N) skeptical", "O) sparking"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) acceptance\n名词\n【词性槽】the ___ of electric cars 作主语\n【依据】the acceptance of electric cars = 电动车的接受度 / 普及，正合上下文\n对应 the __(31)__ of electric cars in the country has not occurred as fast as\nexpected：与下文 Other efforts to increase the use of electric vehicles include plans\nto build over 1 million hybrid and electric car battery changing stations across the\ncountry. 互相呼应——市场对电动车的「接受/普及」没有预期那么快，故政府要采取更多\n举措。\n【竞争词】M) restoration 义不切题\nrestoration「修复、复原」用于古建筑、生态等被破坏后重建的对象，对全新事物电动车没有\n「恢复」一说。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the ___ of electric"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acceptance", "B) currently", "C) disrupting", "D) eliminate", "E) exhaust", "F) futile", "G) hopeful", "H) implemented", "I) incidentally", "J) installed", "K) noticeable", "L) powered", "M) restoration", "N) skeptical", "O) sparking"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) installed\n形容词\n【词性槽】having over 6 million charging stations ___ 中作宾补\n【依据】充电站「被安装好」的搭配最贴切\n对应 By 2030, Germany plans on having over 6 million charging stations __(32)__.：\n充电桩是被安装的对象，install charging stations 是最自然的搭配；having ... stations\ninstalled 表「使充电桩安装完毕」。\n【竞争词】C) disrupting 义反\ndisrupting 是「扰乱的」，让充电桩处于扰乱状态于政策语境完全相反。\nF) futile 义反\nfutile「徒劳的」与全段「扩大充电网络」的积极语气冲突。\nG) hopeful 对象不对\nhopeful 修饰人，不能修饰充电桩。\nH) implemented 搭配对象不对\nimplemented 多与 policy/plan/measure 搭，不与 station 搭。\nK) noticeable 义不切题\nnoticeable「显著的」不解释充电桩状态。\nN) skeptical 对象不对\nskeptical 用于人。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·having over 6 millio"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acceptance", "B) currently", "C) disrupting", "D) eliminate", "E) exhaust", "F) futile", "G) hopeful", "H) implemented", "I) incidentally", "J) installed", "K) noticeable", "L) powered", "M) restoration", "N) skeptical", "O) sparking"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) currently\n副词\n【词性槽】There are ___ + 数词+复数名词，修饰系动词 are\n【依据】对照「现状 vs 未来」，需要「目前」作时间状语\n本段先讲现状 There are __(33)__ around 155,000 registered hybrid and electric\nvehicles on German roads，再用 As countries continue setting goals of reducing\nemissions 转向未来；currently「目前」恰好把这串数据与下文未来目标对照起来。\n【竞争词】I) incidentally 插入语义错位\nincidentally 是「顺便提一句」，多用于话题切换的插入语，与本段「陈述现状以对比未来」的\n语气不合。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·There are ___ + 数词+复"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acceptance", "B) currently", "C) disrupting", "D) eliminate", "E) exhaust", "F) futile", "G) hopeful", "H) implemented", "I) incidentally", "J) installed", "K) noticeable", "L) powered", "M) restoration", "N) skeptical", "O) sparking"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) noticeable\n形容词\n【词性槽】a ___ effect on... 中修饰 effect 作定语\n【依据】have a noticeable effect 高频搭配 + 与 greater steps 呼应\nhave a noticeable/significant effect on... 是常见搭配，对应 greater steps need to be\ntaken to have a __(34)__ effect on the surrounding environment，「采取更大步伐才能\n产生显著影响」逻辑顺。\n【竞争词】C) disrupting 极性反\ndisrupting effect「破坏性影响」与「对环境的积极影响」语境冲突。\nG) hopeful 搭配错位\nhopeful 修饰人或前景，不修饰 effect。\nH) implemented 搭配对象不对\nimplemented 说「被执行的」，不修饰 effect。\nN) skeptical 对象不对\nskeptical 用于人。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a ___ effect on... 中"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) acceptance", "B) currently", "C) disrupting", "D) eliminate", "E) exhaust", "F) futile", "G) hopeful", "H) implemented", "I) incidentally", "J) installed", "K) noticeable", "L) powered", "M) restoration", "N) skeptical", "O) sparking"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) futile\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 + not + ___ 作表语\n【依据】not futile = 不是徒劳的，正合让步语义\nWhile the efforts are certainly not __(35)__, the results of such bans will likely only\nstart to be seen by generations down the line, bettering the world for the future. 形\n成「努力不白费/将造福未来」的肯定基调；futile「徒劳的」最贴让步从句的反义需求。\n【竞争词】C) disrupting 义不切题\ndisrupting「破坏的」与「努力是否值得」的让步无关。\nG) hopeful 极性反\n若填 hopeful，「努力并非有希望的」否定整句基调，与下文「造福未来」矛盾。\nH) implemented 搭配 + 已用倾向\nimplemented 通常说政策「已被执行」，做 efforts 的表语义不通；后面 28 题也更需要它。\nK) noticeable 极性反\nnoticeable 也是正面词，「努力不是显著的」与下句正面预期相悖。\nN) skeptical 对象不对\nskeptical 修饰人或态度，不修饰 efforts。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be 动词 + not + ___ 作表"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "It is a popular belief that tech companies are committed to protecting their customers' private data.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】popular belief + 公司护数据\n【定位】They have our data, and their businesses depend on the global public's collective belief that\nthey will do everything they can to protect that data.\n它们手里握着我们的数据，而它们的生意建立在全球公众的一种集体信念之上——即相信它\n们会竭尽全力保护这些数据。\n【改写】a popular belief ↔ the global public's collective belief\npopular = public 的同义改写\ntech companies are committed to protecting their customers' private data ↔\nthey will do everything they can to protect that data\ncommitted to = do everything they can to\n【辨邻】 H 本段讲 once-lonely privacy advocates find themselves fighting alongside the\nmost powerful company in the world，主语是隐私倡导者与苹果，谈的是品牌与同\n盟，没说 popular belief 这一层「公众集体信念」。\nApple's emerging global brand is privacy\nG 本段 make use of encryption(加密) to limit access to people's data 描述的是苹果\n采取的技术动作，缺少陈述句中 popular belief 这一「公众信念」维度，因此只覆盖\n局部、错位命中。\nby default, make use of encryption(加密) to limit access to people's data", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "The US government believes that its access to people's iPhones could be used to prevent terrorist attacks.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】政府以接触 iPhone 防恐袭\n【定位】Once armed with a method for gaining access to iPhones, the government could ask to use it\nproactively, before a suspected terrorist attack—leaving Apple in a bind as to whether to\ncomply or risk an attack and suffer a public-relations nightmare.\n一旦掌握了进入 iPhone 的手段，政府可能会要求先发制人地使用它，即在疑似恐怖袭击发\n生之前就用——这会让苹果陷入两难：是顺从，还是冒袭击发生与公关噩梦的风险。\n【改写】its access to people's iPhones ↔ armed with a method for gaining access to iPhones\n名词化的 access 对应动词性 gain access\ncould be used to prevent terrorist attacks ↔\ncould ask to use it proactively(先发制⼈地), before a suspected terrorist attack\nbefore 袭击 + proactively = prevent 袭击\n【辨邻】 C 本段 the two attackers who killed 14 people in San Bernardino, California, in\nDecember 说的是袭击已经发生、政府才请求解锁，缺少「在袭击发生前就先用」这\n一前置时间，因此与 prevent terrorist attacks 错位。\nunlock an iPhone used by one of the two attackers who killed 14 people in San Bernardino,\nCalifornia, in December\nF 本段 what is to prevent it from doing so for a request from the Russians or the\nIranians? 谈的是连锁开口子的风险，缺少 prevent terrorist attacks 这一独特概念。\nwhat is to prevent it from doing so for a request from the Russians or the Iranians?", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "A federal court asked Apple to help the FBI access data in a terrorist's iPhone.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】联邦法院 + 帮 FBI 进 iPhone\n【定位】The action stems from a federal court order issued on Tuesday requiring Apple to help the\nFederal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) to unlock an iPhone used by one of the two attackers\nwho killed 14 people in San Bernardino, California, in December.\n此次行动源于周二一道联邦法院命令，该命令要求苹果协助美国联邦调查局解锁圣贝纳迪诺\n枪击案中一名袭击者所使用的 iPhone。\n【改写】A federal court asked Apple to help the FBI ↔\na federal court order issued on Tuesday requiring Apple to help the Federal Bureau of\nInvestigation (FBI)\nasked = requiring 的同义改写\naccess data in a terrorist's iPhone ↔\nunlock an iPhone used by one of the two attackers who killed 14 people\nunlock + attackers 上位概括为 access data + terrorist\n【辨邻】 M 本段 the government could ask to use it proactively(先发制人地), before a\nsuspected terrorist attack 用情态动词 could 表假设，没有 A federal court asked\n这一已经下达的事实动作。\nOnce armed with a method for gaining access to iPhones, the government could ask to use\nit proactively\nD 本段 has said he will appeal the court's order 是对法院命令的反应，没有再次陈述\n命令的内容，属下游动作。\nhas said he will appeal the court's order", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Privacy advocates now have Apple fighting alongside them against government access to personal data.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】隐私倡导者 + 苹果并肩\n【定位】So now, for many cases involving governmental intrusions into data, once-lonely privacy\nadvocates find themselves fighting alongside the most powerful company in the world.\n于是如今，在许多政府入侵数据的案件中，曾经孤军作战的隐私倡导者发现自己正与世界上\n最强大的公司并肩作战。\n【改写】Privacy advocates now have Apple fighting alongside them ↔\nonce-lonely privacy advocates find themselves fighting alongside the most powerful company\nin the world\n视角调换的同义重写\nagainst government access to personal data ↔ governmental intrusions into data\nintrusions 上位 = access 反向同义\n【辨邻】 B 本段 tech giants began to recognize the United States government as a hostile\nactor 缺少 Privacy advocates 这一独有角色，因此话题相邻但不命中。\ntech giants began to recognize the United States government as a hostile actor\nG 本段 made their opposition to the government's claims a point of corporate\npride 立场虽与陈述句一致，但缺少 Privacy advocates 这一独有角色。\nmade their opposition to the government's claims a point of corporate pride", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Snowden revealed that the American government had tried hard to access private data on a massive scale.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】斯诺登 + 政府大规模取数据\n【定位】After revelations by the former National Security Agency contractor Edward J. Snowden in\n2013 that the government both cozied up to certain tech companies and hacked into others to\ngain access to private data on an enormous scale, tech giants began to recognize the United\nStates government as a hostile actor.\n在前国家安全局承包商爱德华·斯诺登于 2013 年揭露政府既讨好部分科技公司、又黑入其他\n公司以前所未有的规模获取私人数据之后，科技巨头开始把美国政府视为敌对方。\n【改写】Snowden revealed ↔\nrevelations by the former National Security Agency contractor Edward J. Snowden in 2013\n动词 revealed = 名词 revelations\ntried hard to access private data on a massive scale ↔\nhacked into others to gain access to private data on an enormous scale\nhacked + gain access = tried hard to access\n【辨邻】 G 本段 Apple's stance on these issues emerged post-Snowden 中 Snowden 只承担\n「之后」这一时间状语，没陈述他「揭露了什么」。\nApple's stance on these issues emerged post-Snowden", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "The FBI might have been able to access private data in earlier iPhones without Apple's help.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】早期 iPhone + FBI 无需苹果帮助\n【定位】And to get at the contents of a single iPhone, the government says it needs a court order and\nApple's help to write new code; in earlier versions of the iPhone, ones that were created\nbefore Apple found religion on privacy, the FBI might have been able to break into the device\nby itself.\n为了取得单部 iPhone 的内容，政府称它需要一份法院命令以及苹果的帮助来写新代码；而\n在更早期的 iPhone 上——即在苹果热衷隐私之前生产的那些——FBI 本可能自己就能闯入\n这部设备。\n【改写】earlier iPhones ↔ earlier versions of the iPhone\n复数 iPhones = versions of the iPhone\naccess private data ... without Apple's help ↔\nthe FBI might have been able to break into the device by itself\nbreak into + by itself = access ... without ... help\n【辨邻】 K 本段 measures that it could put into place in the future will almost certainly be\nable to further limit the government's reach 把方向定为「未来 + 更收紧」，与陈\n述句的「过去 + FBI 能自己破」时间方向相反。\nmeasures that it could put into place in the future will almost certainly be able to further limit the\ngovernment's reach\nC 本段 requiring Apple to help the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) to unlock\nan iPhone 谈的是本案「必须靠苹果」，与陈述句「早期不必靠苹果」在时间维度上\n对立。\nrequiring Apple to help the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) to unlock an iPhone", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "After the Snowden incident, Apple made clear its position to counter government intrusion into personal data by means of encryption.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】斯诺登事件后 + 加密对抗政府\n【定位】Apple's stance on these issues emerged post-Snowden, when the company started putting in\nplace a series of technologies that, by default, make use of encryption to limit access to\npeople's data.\n苹果在这些议题上的立场出现在斯诺登事件之后——当时该公司开始部署一系列默认采用加\n密的技术，以限制对人们数据的访问。\n【改写】After the Snowden incident ↔ post-Snowden\nafter = post 同义改写\nApple made clear its position to counter government intrusion ↔\nApple's stance on these issues emerged post-Snowden\nstance emerged = made clear position\nby means of encryption ↔ make use of encryption(加密) to limit access to people's data\nby means of = make use of 同义改写\n【辨邻】 B 本段 tech giants began to recognize the United States government as a hostile\nactor 只讲态度，没有 by means of encryption 这一具体手段。\ntech giants began to recognize the United States government as a hostile actor\nH 本段 Apple's emerging global brand is privacy 是品牌层面的陈述，缺少 by means\nof encryption 这一手段词。\nApple's emerging global brand is privacy", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "According to one digital expert, no iPhone can be entirely free from hacking.", "options": null, "answer": "P", "analysis": "【答案】P 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】数字专家 + 没 iPhone 完全免黑\n【定位】\"Nothing is 100 percent hacker-proof,\" Mr. Zdziarski said, but he pointed out that the judge's\norder in this case required Apple to provide \"reasonable security assistance\" to unlock Mr.\nFarook's phone.\nZdziarski 说，没有任何东西是百分之百防黑的，但他指出本案的法官命令要求苹果提供合\n理的安全协助，以解锁 Farook 的手机。\n【改写】According to one digital expert ↔ Mr. Zdziarski said\n数字专家 = Zdziarski 引出方\nno iPhone can be entirely free from hacking ↔ Nothing is 100 percent hacker-proof\n100 percent hacker-proof = entirely free from hacking\n【辨邻】 N 本段 I bet they are rethinking their threat model as we speak 是 Zdziarski 关于威\n胁模型重思的观点，没有 no iPhone can be entirely free from hacking 这一含义。\nI bet they are rethinking their threat model as we speak", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Timothy Cook's long web post has helped enhance Apple's image.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】库克长帖 + 抬高苹果形象\n【定位】\"Then you had a few companies involved, but not one of the largest companies in the world\ncoming out with a lengthy and impassioned post, like we saw yesterday from Timothy Cook.\nIts profile has really been raised.\"\n他说，那时也有几家公司卷入，但没有一家像我们昨天看到的蒂姆·库克这样、来自世界上最\n大公司之一、发出一篇长篇又激情澎湃的网帖。它的知名度真的被抬高了。\n【改写】Timothy Cook's long web post ↔\na lengthy and impassioned post, like we saw yesterday from Timothy Cook\nlong = lengthy 同义改写\nhelped enhance Apple's image ↔ Its profile has really been raised\nprofile raised = image enhanced\n【辨邻】 D 本段 Timothy Cook, has said he will appeal the court's order 是关于上诉的动作，\n没出现 long web post 与 enhance Apple's image。\nTimothy Cook, has said he will appeal the court's order", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Apple's CEO has decided to appeal the federal court's order to unlock a user's iPhone.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】苹果 CEO + 上诉法院命令\n【定位】In the other corner is the world's most valuable company, whose chief executive, Timothy\nCook, has said he will appeal the court's order.\n另一角站的是世界上最有价值的公司，其首席执行官蒂姆·库克已表示将对法院命令提出\n上诉。\n【改写】Apple's CEO ↔ chief executive, Timothy Cook\nCEO = chief executive 同义\nappeal the federal court's order to unlock a user's iPhone ↔\nhas said he will appeal the court's order\nwill appeal = has decided to appeal\n【辨邻】 I 本段 a lengthy and impassioned post, like we saw yesterday from Timothy Cook\n谈库克的长帖与知名度抬升，没有 appeal 这一独特动词。\na lengthy and impassioned post, like we saw yesterday from Timothy Cook\nC 本段 a federal court order issued on Tuesday requiring Apple to help the\nFederal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) to unlock an iPhone 讲的是命令本身，主语\n是法院；陈述句主语是苹果 CEO，动作是上诉。\na federal court order issued on Tuesday requiring Apple to help the Federal Bureau of Investigation\n(FBI) to unlock an iPhone\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 开篇定调：苹果与执法部门就解锁恐怖分子手机的对峙，是科技业与美国政府之间力\n量天平慢慢翻转的顶点。\n信息指纹 力量翻转的顶点\nB 斯诺登 2013 年揭露政府讨好部分科技公司、又黑入其他公司以大规模获取私人数据\n后，科技巨头开始把美国政府视为敌对方；长远看科技公司必胜。\n信息指纹 斯诺登 2013 揭露\nC 联邦法院周二下令要求苹果协助 FBI 解锁圣贝纳迪诺枪击案凶手之一使用的 iPhone。\n信息指纹 联邦法院命令 + 圣贝纳迪诺案\nD\n苹果 CEO 蒂姆·库克表示将对法院命令提出上诉；苹果主张：削弱单部 iPhone 会让所\n有 iPhone 面临被任何政府入侵者攻破的风险。\n信息指纹 库克声明上诉\nE 胜负未卜，但底层逻辑很简单：苹果、谷歌、Facebook 等手握用户数据，其业务依赖\n于公众相信它们会尽全力保护数据。\n信息指纹 公众相信会尽力护数据\nF 一旦在保护数据这条战线上出现裂缝，对必须在全球运营的科技公司是致命的——为\n美国开了口子，俄罗斯、伊朗也会照样要求。\n信息指纹 全球开口子的连锁后果\nG 斯诺登事件后，苹果开始默认部署一系列加密技术来限制对用户数据的访问，并把反\n对政府主张作为企业骄傲。\n信息指纹 斯诺登事件后默认加密\nH 隐私已成为苹果的全球品牌；如今在不少涉政府入侵数据的案子里，原本孤军作战的\n隐私倡导者发现自己与全球最强公司并肩作战。\n信息指纹 孤军隐私倡导者与苹果并肩\nI 电子前沿基金会总顾问 Kurt Opsahl 将本案与 1990 年代加密之战对比：当年没有像库\n克这样来自全球最大公司之一的长篇激情贴文，事件知名度因此被大大抬高。\n信息指纹 库克长贴抬高知名度\nJ 苹果手里还有一张王牌：用技术手段让设备越来越难被进入。当下取 iPhone 内容要\n靠法院令加苹果写新代码；而在更早期的 iPhone 上，FBI 本可能不靠苹果自己破解。\n信息指纹 早期 iPhone 上 FBI 可自破\nK 无论本案输赢，苹果未来还能再加码：把政府的触手限制得更死。\n信息指纹 未来措施继续收紧束缚\nL 本案结果并非无足轻重：迫使苹果写软件给 FBI 开口子会形成令人不安的先例，可能\n被援引到与国家安全无关的其他调查上。\n信息指纹 形成令人不安的先例\nM 一旦掌握进入 iPhone 的手段，政府可能在嫌疑恐袭发生前先发制人地使用；Opsahl\n称这是绕开后门之争的迂回战术——直接下令制造后门。\n信息指纹 先发制人 + 直接下令造后门\nN 即便苹果最终输掉本案，它在技术上仍有大量手段随时间关闭后门；数字专家\nJonathan Zdziarski 称苹果工程师此刻应已在重新思考威胁模型。\n信息指纹 工程师重思威胁模型\nO Zdziarski 给出一种相对简单的对策：让未来的 iPhone 要求用户先输入密码，才能接\n受 FBI 想让苹果造的那种修改版操作系统；这样苹果就无法单方面装上削弱手机的\n代码。\n信息指纹 用户输密码后才接受改版系统\nP Zdziarski 强调没有任何东西百分之百防黑；若苹果让未来 iPhone 连自家工程师的合\n理协助都破不开，那么本案先例的长期效力就会失去——FBI 即便赢了本案，长期看\n也是输。\n信息指纹 没东西百分之百防黑", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What can we learn about Lake Natron?", "options": ["A) It is simply uninhabitable for most animals.", "B) It remains little known to the outside world.", "C) It is a breeding ground for a variety of birds.", "D) It makes an ideal habitat for lots of predators."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What can we learn about Lake Natron 没有 infer/suggest/imply 等推断词，也没有问\nmainly about/best title，要求的是可从原文直接读出的关于该湖的事实。\n【拆句】What can we learn about Lake Natron 是典型一般疑问的特殊问句结构，主干即 we can\nlearn what about Lake Natron，无从句、无否定、无比较，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Bats, swallows and more are chemically preserved in the pose in which they perished, sealed\nin the deposits of sodium carbonate in the water. The lake's landscape is bizarre and deadly—\nand made even more so by the fact that it's the place where nearly 75 percent of the world's\nflamingos are born.\n蝙蝠、燕子等动物以它们死亡时的姿势被化学性地保存下来，封存于水中的碳酸钠沉积物\n里。这片湖的景象既奇异又致命——而且全球近 75% 的火烈鸟都在此出生这一事实让它\n更甚。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nThe lake's landscape is bizarre and deadly ↔ It is simply uninhabitable for most animals\nBats, swallows and more are chemically preserved in the pose in which they perished ↔\nfor most animals\n定位句两层信息——动物大量死亡 + 湖本身致命——分别对应选项的 most animals 和\nuninhabitable，两层全部命中，锁定 A。\nB) 无中生有靠扫雷\nremains little known to the outside world 谈的是该湖对外界的知名度，文章自始至\n终未涉及外界是否知道这座湖。\nC) 数量范围被放大即误\n原文只说 nearly 75 percent of the world's flamingos are born，仅指火烈鸟一种；选\n项 a breeding ground for a variety of birds 把单一鸟种夸大成 a variety of birds（多\n种鸟），与原文不符。\nD) 条件性句子被绝对化\n原文谈到捕食者是在第 2 段——predators can move briskly across the lake bed\nand attack，且明确这是水位过低时的情况，捕食者并不是把湖当作理想栖息地；选项\nan ideal habitat for lots of predators 与原文意思相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "Flamingos nest only when the lake water is at a specific level so that their babies can____.", "options": ["A) find safe shelter more easily", "B) grow thick feathers on their feet", "C) stay away from predators", "D) get accustomed to the salty water"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Flamingos nest only when the lake water is at a specific level so that their babies\ncan 是 so that 引导的目的状语待补全，问的是特定水位带来的具体好处，是直接可在原文\n找到的事实。\n【拆句】Flamingos nest only when the lake water is at a specific level so that their babies\ncan 含两层从句：only when 引导时间状语限定筑巢条件，so that 引导目的状语句子未\n完。理解关键是抓住 so that 后的内容才是待选的目的部分，不要被前面的条件分句带偏。\n【定位】Three-quarters of the world's flamingos fly over from other salt lakes in the Rift Valley and\nnest on salt-crystal islands that appear when the water is at a specific level—too high and the\nbirds can't build their nests, too low and predators can move briskly across the lake bed and\nattack. When the water hits the right level, the baby birds are kept safe from predators by a\ncorrosive ditch.\n全球四分之三的火烈鸟从裂谷其他盐湖飞来，在水位达到某一特定水平时浮现的盐晶岛上筑\n巢——水位过高鸟无法筑巢，水位过低则捕食者可在湖床上迅速穿越发起袭击。当水位恰到\n好处时，幼鸟由一道腐蚀性的水沟与捕食者隔开而得以保全。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴跨段挪用\n原文 Fish have a refuge in the streams 提到的 refuge 是给罗非鱼的，不是给火烈鸟\n幼鸟的；find safe shelter more easily 把这一信息张冠李戴到火烈鸟身上。\nB) 偷换概念混名词\n原文 Flamingos have evolved very leathery skin on their legs 讲的是 leathery skin\n（皮革般皮肤）且部位在 legs（腿）；选项 grow thick feathers on their feet 把皮肤\n改成羽毛、把腿改成脚，且 grow 是后天动作，与 have evolved 的进化属性不符。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe baby birds are kept safe from predators by a corrosive ditch ↔ stay away from predators\n预判要求选项含 '远离捕食者' 的语义，四个选项里只有 C 直接复现 predators 一词，且\nstay away from 与 kept safe from 等值，锁定 C。\nD) 细节题看主语是谁\n原文谈适应盐水的是成年火烈鸟（tolerate the salt water），并不是题干所问的幼鸟；\nget accustomed to the salty water 把幼鸟当成适应主体，主体错位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Flamingos in the Rift Valley are unique in that_____.", "options": ["A) they can move swiftly across lagoons", "B) they can survive well in salty water", "C) they breed naturally in corrosive ditches", "D) they know where and when to nest"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Flamingos in the Rift Valley are unique in that 问的是火烈鸟独特性的具体表现，可直接\n由原文中的对比句得出，不需多步推理。\n【拆句】Flamingos in the Rift Valley are unique in that 是 unique in that 结构，相当于 unique\nbecause，that 引导原因状语从句而非定语从句，从句句子尚未完整，待选项补齐。\n【定位】Flamingos, however, are the only species that actually makes life in the midst of all\nthat death.\n然而，火烈鸟是唯一一种真正能在那一片死寂之中扎根生活的物种。\n【选项】A) 动作主语被抢\n原文 predators can move briskly across the lake bed and attack 中 move briskly\n的主语是 predators（捕食者），不是火烈鸟；they can move swiftly across lagoons\n把这一动作误归给火烈鸟，且把 lake bed（湖床）改写为 lagoons（泻湖）。\nB) ✓ 正确\nare the only species that actually makes life in the midst of all that death ↔\nthey can survive well in salty water\nFlamingos have evolved very leathery skin on their legs so they can tolerate the salt water ↔\nin salty water\n预判要求选项体现 '唯一能在腐蚀盐湖生活' 的特性，B 既覆盖 survive 又点出 salty\nwater 介质，命中唯一性的核心，锁定 B。\nC) 功能误读为场所\n原文 the baby birds are kept safe from predators by a corrosive ditch 中腐蚀沟是\n'隔离屏障' 而非火烈鸟的繁殖场所；选项 they breed naturally in corrosive ditches 把\n屏障误读为繁殖地，与原文意思相反。\nD) 客观行为拟人化即过推\n原文只说火烈鸟在特定水位时筑巢，并未说它们 '知道在哪里、何时筑巢'；they know\nwhere and when to nest 把客观事件拟人化为火烈鸟的认知能力，属过度推断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "Why can certain species of tilapia sometimes survive around Lake Natron?", "options": ["A) They can take refuge in the less salty waters.", "B) They can flee quick enough from predators.", "C) They can move freely from lagoon to lagoon.", "D) They can stand the heat of the spring water."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why can certain species of tilapia sometimes survive around Lake Natron 虽以 Why\n提问，但原因可直接在原文中找到对应句，不需推断。\n【拆句】Why can certain species of tilapia sometimes survive around Lake Natron 是\nWhy+can+主语+sometimes+谓语 的疑问句，sometimes 是关键副词，限定 survive 的\n频率，提示并非任意时刻都能存活，必有前提条件。\n【定位】Some fish, too, have had limited success vacationing at the lake as less salty lagoons form on\nthe outer edges from hot springs flowing into Lake Natron. Three species of tilapia thrive\nthere part-time. \"Fish have a refuge in the streams and can expand into the lagoons when the\nlake is low and the lagoons are separate,\" Harper said.\n一些鱼也算在湖里 '度假' 略有小成——温泉注入 Lake Natron 时，在湖外缘形成了盐度较\n低的泻湖。三种罗非鱼在那里季节性地繁衍。Harper 说：'鱼可以以溪流为庇护，在湖水\n低、泻湖彼此隔开时扩展到泻湖里去。'\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nless salty lagoons form on the outer edges from hot springs flowing into Lake Natron ↔\nThey can take refuge in the less salty waters\nFish have a refuge in the streams ↔ take refuge\n预判要求选项体现 '可退守较淡水域'，A 把 streams/lagoons 概括为 less salty\nwaters，并把 refuge 保留为核心词，命中预判，锁定 A。\nB) 概念跨段挪用\n原文谈罗非鱼时只提水位与盐度，未涉及捕食者；flee quick enough from predators\n把第 2 段火烈鸟相关的 predators 概念挪用到罗非鱼身上，属张冠李戴。\nC) 条件状语句易被反读\n原文 All the lagoons join when the lake is high and fish must retreat to their\nstream refuges or die 明确写道：水位高时泻湖才合并，但此时鱼必须撤回溪流否则会\n死；选项 move freely from lagoon to lagoon 把这一危急情形误读为鱼可自由游动，\n与原文相反。\nD) 背景信息被读成原因\n原文 hot springs flowing into Lake Natron 中 hot springs 只是泻湖形成的来源，未\n说罗非鱼以耐热为生存机制；stand the heat of the spring water 把背景信息夸大为存\n活原因，属过度推断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What may be the consequence of Tanzanian government's planned operation?", "options": ["A) The accelerated extinction of flamingos.", "B) The change of flamingos' migration route.", "C) The overmining of Lake Natron's soda ash.", "D) The disruption of Lake Natron's ecosystem."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What may be the consequence of Tanzanian government's planned operation 问的\n是可能后果，原文里有专家直接表态的 worry 句作答，是直陈细节而非考生需自行推断。\n【拆句】What may be the consequence of Tanzanian government's planned operation 主干\n是 What may be the consequence，consequence of 后接两层所有格修饰（政府的+计\n划的），may 是模态动词表可能性，是理解题干的关键。\n【定位】Although the planned operation will be located more than 40 miles away, drawing the soda\nash in through pipelines, conservationists worry it could still upset the natural water cycle and\nbreeding grounds.\n尽管计划中的开采项目将设在 40 多英里以外，通过管道抽取苏打粉，但环保人士担忧这仍\n可能扰乱该湖的自然水循环和火烈鸟的繁殖地。\n【选项】A) 局部影响升级为终极后果\n原文仅言担忧扰乱 breeding grounds（繁殖地），未涉及火烈鸟物种灭绝；The\naccelerated extinction of flamingos 把局部影响夸大为物种灭绝，属过度推断。\nB) 新维度选项即无中生有\n原文未提任何关于火烈鸟迁徙路线变化的信息，仅提及它们从其他盐湖飞来繁殖；The\nchange of flamingos' migration route 引入原文未涉及的维度，属无中生有。\nC) 前缀加 over- 即过度推断\n原文只描述了开采计划的距离与方式，未给出 '过度开采' 的信号；The overmining of\nLake Natron's soda ash 把 mining 升级为 overmining，属无中生有。\nD) ✓ 正确\nupset the natural water cycle and breeding grounds ↔\nThe disruption of Lake Natron's ecosystem\n预判要求选项体现 '扰乱湖的自然水循环和繁殖地' 的后果，D 用 disruption 名词化原文\nupset，再用 ecosystem 概括 water cycle 与 breeding grounds，是同义改写的标准\n范式，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do most employees plan to do towards the end of the year?", "options": ["A) Go for a vacation.", "B) Keep on working.", "C) Set an objective for next year.", "D) Review the year's achievements."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do most employees plan to do towards the end of the year? 问的是一个具体行\n为事实，没有 infer/suggest/imply，属于典型的事实细节询问。\n【拆句】题干主干 What do most employees plan to do 是 do 引导的疑问句，towards the end\nof the year 是时间状语后置，结构平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【定位】More of them, though, will skip those calculations altogether and just power through the\nholidays into 2017: More than half of American workers don't use up all of their allotted\nvacation days each year.\n不过更多的员工会干脆跳过这些核算，硬扛着把节假日推进到 2017 年：超过一半的美国员\n工每年并没有用完所有获得的休假日。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nGo for a vacation 与定位句 don't use up all of their allotted vacation days 的语义\n正相反——原文恰恰是说大多数人不去度假。\nB) ✓ 正确\npower through the holidays into 2017 ↔ Keep on working\ndon't use up all of their allotted vacation days ↔ Keep on working\n预判要求 \"年末继续工作\"，Keep on working 是这一语义最直接、最不绕弯的表述。\nC) 无中生有\nSet an objective for next year 提到的 \"为明年定目标\" 在原文中没有任何对应——原\n文只说算剩多少假、然后继续干活，没说任何制定计划的事。\nD) 无中生有\nReview the year's achievements 谈的是 \"年终回顾成绩\"，原文只在算休假天数\n（math），从未涉及业绩盘点。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "How would people view dedication to work in the past?", "options": ["A) They would regard it as a matter of course.", "B) They would consider it a must for success.", "C) They would look upon it with contempt.", "D) They would deem it a trick of businessmen."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】How would people view dedication to work in the past? 问的是过去人们对一件具体事\n情的看法，是可在原文找到出处的事实细节。\n【拆句】How would people view dedication to work in the past? 中 would 表过去习惯，view\n是及物动词，宾语 dedication to work 后接时间状语，无难点。\n【定位】Not so long ago, people would have turned up their noses at that kind of dedication to the job.\n不久以前，人们对那种对工作的过度投入是嗤之以鼻的。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nregard it as a matter of course 表示 \"视为理所当然\"，意味着被默认接受；但定位句\nturned up their noses at 表达的是嗤之以鼻、不接受。\nB) 无中生有\nconsider it a must for success 说过去把忙工作视作成功必经之路；原文从未涉及\nsuccess / necessary 这一组语义。\nC) ✓ 正确\nturned up their noses at ↔ look upon it with contempt\n预判要 \"看不起\"，contempt 是英语里最直接的 \"鄙视\"，C 是唯一与定位句负面色彩对\n应的选项。\nD) 无中生有\ndeem it a trick of businessmen 说过去把忙工作视作商人的把戏；原文既没出现\nbusinessmen，也没把这种 dedication 归为任何 trick。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What did the researchers find through a series of experiments?", "options": ["A) The busier one appears, the more respect one earns.", "B) The more one works, the more one feels exploited.", "C) The more knowledge one has, the more competent one will be.", "D) The higher one's status, the more vacation time one will enjoy."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What did the researchers find through a series of experiments? 问的是研究者发现的\n具体结论，是事实型细节。\n【拆句】主句 What did the researchers find 是一般疑问句的特殊疑问形式，through a series of\nexperiments 是方式状语，无障碍。\n【定位】In a series of several experiments, the researchers illustrated just how much we've come to\nadmire busyness, or at least the appearance of it.\n在一系列实验中，研究者们展示了我们已经多么推崇忙碌，至少是推崇忙碌的外在表现。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nwe've come to admire busyness, or at least the appearance of it ↔\nThe busier one appears, the more respect one earns\ncustomers of the grocery-delivery service Peapod were seen as of higher status ↔\nThe busier one appears, the more respect one earns\nthe + 比较级，the + 比较级结构精准捕捉到原文 \"程度越深→社会评价越高\" 的因果，\n且 \"appears\" 一词精确对应 the appearance of it——这种对 appearance 的精准吸收\n是 A 优于其他选项的关键。\nB) 无中生有\nThe more one works, the more one feels exploited 引入 \"被剥削感\"——原文从未涉\n及任何 exploitation 类负面情绪，反而强调忙碌带来正面评价。\nC) 答非所问\nThe more knowledge one has, the more competent one will be 谈知识与能力的关\n系——这是后文 Q54 的话题，不是本题实验的发现。\nD) 与原文相反\nThe higher one's status, the more vacation time one will enjoy 把 \"地位与休假\" 正\n向关联——但原文实验恰恰说 \"高地位者反而看起来更忙、休假更少\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What may account for the change of people's attitude towards being busy?", "options": ["A) The fast pace of life in modern society.", "B) The fierce competition in the job market.", "C) The widespread use of computer technology.", "D) The role of knowledge in modern economy."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What may account for the change of people's attitude towards being busy? 问的是变\n化的具体原因，原因可在原文找到出处，不必跨段推理。\n【拆句】主干 What may account for the change，of people's attitude 与 towards being busy\n是两层后置定语，逐层修饰 change，需注意修饰链不要断在 attitude 上。\n【定位】We think that the shift from leisure-as-status to busyness-as-status may be linked to the\ndevelopment of knowledge-intensive economics.\n我们认为，地位象征从闲暇到忙碌的转变，可能与知识密集型经济的发展有关。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThe fast pace of life in modern society 谈 \"现代生活节奏快\"——原文并没有把忙碌\n归因于生活节奏，只归因于经济形态。\nB) 偷换概念\nThe fierce competition in the job market 谈的是 \"就业市场的激烈竞争\"——原文 in\nhigh demand and short supply on the job market 强调的是稀缺与抢手（卖方市\n场），不是 \"竞争激烈\"。\nC) 无中生有\nThe widespread use of computer technology 谈电脑技术普及——原文从未提及\ncomputer / technology 这一组概念。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe development of knowledge-intensive economics ↔\nThe role of knowledge in modern economy\nindividuals who possess the human capital characteristics that employers or clients value (e.g.,\ncompetence and ambition) are expected to be in high demand and short supply\n↔ The role of knowledge in modern economy\n预判要求原因是 \"知识密集型经济\"，四个选项里只有 D 把 knowledge 与 economy 直\n接绑定，其他选项要么换主词、要么换领域，均不与原文原因端对齐。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author advise us to do at the end of the passage?", "options": ["A) Schedule our time properly for efficiency.", "B) Plan our weekends in a meaningful way.", "C) Find time to relax however busy we are.", "D) Avoid appearing busy when we are not."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author advise us to do at the end of the passage? 已指明位置在最后一\n段，问作者的具体建议，是单点细节询问而非全文主旨。\n【拆句】What does the author advise us to do 主干清晰，at the end of the passage 是元信息\n状语，提示在文末找答案，本身不入选项语义。\n【定位】Even if you feel tempted to sacrifice your own vacation days for fake busyness, though, at\nleast consider leaving your weekends unscheduled. It's for your own good.\n即便你想为 \"假装忙碌\" 牺牲自己的假期，那也至少考虑把周末空出来不安排事情。这是为\n你自己好。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nSchedule our time properly for efficiency 提 \"为效率合理安排时间\"——原文从未把\n建议落在 \"efficiency\"，恰恰相反，作者建议把周末 unscheduled。\nB) 偷换概念\nPlan our weekends in a meaningful way 表面与 weekends 词面相符，但\n\"meaningful 地安排周末\" 是给周末加内容，原文恰恰建议 leaving your weekends\nunscheduled —— 不安排。\nC) ✓ 正确\nat least consider leaving your weekends unscheduled ↔\nFind time to relax however busy we are\nEven if you feel tempted to sacrifice your own vacation days for fake busyness ↔\nFind time to relax however busy we are\n预判要 \"无论多忙都要给自己留休息时间\"，C 用 however busy + find time to relax\n精准捕捉这一含义。\nD) 答非所问\nAvoid appearing busy when we are not 与全文 \"假装忙碌\" 的批评是相关的，但末段\n作者的具体建议不是 \"别装忙\"，而是 \"无论真忙假忙，至少给自己留休息\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2017-12-1"});
